EXT. ICE CAVE ENTRANCE - GLACIER - DAWN

Blue-white. Wind. Cliffs of ice stacked like cathedral organ pipes.

Five figures in orange survival suits crawl from a snow tunnel. Frost in their beards. Smoke from a flare rising behind them.

EZEKIEL "ZEKE" TALLMAN, 40s, leader, hauls a rope. ANNELIESE VOSS-HARTE, 30s, glaciologist, clutches a sample case. RAFFERTY, 50s, a gruff guide, checks his compass. MAHINA, 20s, the youngest, coughs into her glove. A SURVIVOR, 40s, who joined them on the ice, trails behind in a torn red anorak.

ZEKE
Rest. Ten minutes.

ANNELIESE
We can't. Whiteout's coming.

RAFFERTY
She's right.

ZEKE
Ten.

Mahina sits. Anneliese opens the case.

ANNELIESE
Cores are intact.

MAHINA
Good.

The Survivor limps up. Stops. Looks at their faces.

SURVIVOR
You have food?

ZEKE
A little.

SURVIVOR
Water?

RAFFERTY
Melt snow.

SURVIVOR
Lighter?

Rafferty tosses one. The man catches it, thanks him with a nod, and sits apart.

ZEKE
What's your name?

SURVIVOR
Corwin.

ZEKE
How'd you end up out here, Corwin?

CORWIN
Plane. Two days ago. Engines quit.

MAHINA
There were others?

CORWIN
Yes.

ANNELIESE
Where?

Corwin gestures north.

CORWIN
Gone.

RAFFERTY
(low, to Zeke)
No wreck on the radar. I checked.

Zeke studies Corwin. The man is feeding twigs to a tiny fire, humming.

ZEKE
(to Corwin)
Show me your hands.

CORWIN
Why?

ZEKE
Humor me.

Corwin opens his gloves. His fingers are clean, unfrostbitten, pink as a child's.

MAHINA
He's been out here two days?

Corwin's humming stops. He closes his hands, rises smoothly, and the limp is gone.

CORWIN
Not two days.
